course: dictionary
lesson: 15527
title: Successfully: how well something went
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Successfully is an adverb. It tells you how something happened, and it links to a goal or plan. If you did something successfully, you achieved what you wanted or planned to do. «She successfully finished her spelling test.» She wanted to finish the test, and she did.
explain: Successfully can also mean without failing or going wrong. «The experiment worked successfully.» The scientist planned an experiment, and it went right. «File saved successfully» on a computer means the file was kept safely, with no problem.
explain: Look for clues in the sentence. Ask: did something go to plan? If the answer is yes, successfully probably fits. Then try swapping in the word well or correctly to check that the meaning still makes sense.
fact: Successfully means in a way that achieves what you wanted or planned.
fact: Successfully also means without failing or going wrong.
fact: «She successfully scored 7 of her 10 shots.» This means 7 shots went in as she planned.
fact: You will often see successfully in school tasks, science reports and on computer screens.
fact: Successfully is an adverb, so it tells you how an action was done, like how a test was finished.
exercise: «The team successfully finished the race.» What does successfully mean here? | *They finished and achieved what they wanted | They ran backwards | They stopped to eat | They got lost on the way
exercise: Dad sees a message on the screen: «File saved successfully.» What does it tell him? | *The file was saved without going wrong | The file was made into a game | The file was lost and deleted | The file is too big to open
exercise: «She successfully scored 7 of her 10 shots.» Find and copy the word that tells you how the shots went. | scored | ten | *successfully | shots
test: «The scientist's experiment worked successfully.» What does successfully mean here? | It was done in secret | It failed and stopped halfway | *It worked the way it was planned | It was very loud and noisy
test: Maya says, «I successfully lost my bag.» Why is this sentence wrong? | Bags cannot be lost | It is correct, because successfully means lost | Successfully is only used for animals | *Losing a bag is not something you plan to achieve, so successfully does not fit
exam: Which sentence uses successfully correctly? | The dog successfully fell off the bed. | He successfully forgot his book at home. | *She successfully finished her spelling test with no mistakes. | The cake successfully burnt in the oven.
